WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.300
When every test from a pond comes back negative, what have you learned?

2
00:00:08.640 --> 00:00:09.660
Is the species gone?

3
00:00:11.000 --> 00:00:15.520
At 53 English ponds, newts were seen while the water was being sampled.

4
00:00:16.860 --> 00:00:20.860
Yet 13% % of those samples, about seven, showed no newt DNA.

5
00:00:24.683 --> 00:00:27.143
A survey is a series of nets with holes.

6
00:00:28.603 --> 00:00:30.963
First, DNA must be in the water you scoop.

7
00:00:32.203 --> 00:00:33.523
That chance is theta.

8
00:00:34.783 --> 00:00:36.903
Second, the extraction must capture it.

9
00:00:37.983 --> 00:00:39.083
That chance is gamma.

10
00:00:40.323 --> 00:00:43.343
Third, one qPCR well must amplify it.

11
00:00:44.443 --> 00:00:45.563
That chance is delta.

12
00:00:46.733 --> 00:00:49.013
Together, the nets give one number, q.

13
00:00:50.153 --> 00:00:55.093
It is the chance that one water sample detects a pond that really is occupied.

14
00:00:56.453 --> 00:00:58.733
Here we use a round number: q is one half.

15
00:00:59.833 --> 00:01:02.033
Now picture a hundred ponds like yours.

16
00:01:03.413 --> 00:01:06.393
Before sampling, we think twenty of them hold the species.

17
00:01:07.753 --> 00:01:12.053
Those are teal. The other eighty, in grey, are empty.

18
00:01:13.313 --> 00:01:17.053
With no false positives, an empty pond always tests negative.

19
00:01:18.217 --> 00:01:20.097
Sample every pond once.

20
00:01:21.417 --> 00:01:25.137
Half the occupied ponds light up as detections and leave the pile.

21
00:01:26.497 --> 00:01:29.977
Ten occupied ponds stay hidden among eighty empty ones.

22
00:01:31.117 --> 00:01:35.137
The share of negative ponds still occupied drops to eleven percent.

23
00:01:36.267 --> 00:01:41.367
Now take a second, independent sample from every pond that is still negative.

24
00:01:42.747 --> 00:01:45.007
How many hidden occupied ponds will remain?

25
00:01:46.167 --> 00:01:49.247
And what share of the negative ponds will still hold the species?

26
00:01:55.933 --> 00:01:57.973
Half of the ten hidden ponds are caught.

27
00:01:59.433 --> 00:02:02.353
Five stay hidden, among 85 negative ponds.

28
00:02:03.493 --> 00:02:05.033
The share falls to about 6%.

29
00:02:05.033 --> 00:02:10.793
percent. A third sample leaves 2.5 hidden ponds on average, about 3%.

30
00:02:10.793 --> 00:02:11.793
percent.

31
00:02:12.233 --> 00:02:16.513
The side bar tracks the chance that a negative pond is still occupied.

32
00:02:17.893 --> 00:02:21.593
It falls with every independent sample, but never reaches zero.

33
00:02:23.617 --> 00:02:25.217
The grid is the equation.

34
00:02:26.537 --> 00:02:31.937
On top sit the hidden occupied ponds: the prior, times the chance an occupied pond

35
00:02:31.937 --> 00:02:33.137
misses every time.

36
00:02:34.697 --> 00:02:38.657
Below sit all the negative ponds: the empty ones, plus those hidden ones.

37
00:02:39.650 --> 00:02:43.310
Two numbers here are set by us, not measured by this survey.

38
00:02:44.610 --> 00:02:48.250
One is the prior, how likely occupancy was before sampling.

39
00:02:49.550 --> 00:02:52.650
The other is the stop line, here five percent.

40
00:02:52.650 --> 00:02:55.690
Change either, and the samples you need change.

41
00:02:56.800 --> 00:02:59.440
The count also assumes no false positives.

42
00:03:00.880 --> 00:03:04.080
Each pond stays occupied or empty for the whole survey.

43
00:03:05.360 --> 00:03:07.520
And each sample is an independent try.

44
00:03:08.550 --> 00:03:10.770
So a negative never proves absence.

45
00:03:12.190 --> 00:03:16.230
Each negative sample multiplies the hidden ponds by the miss chance.

46
00:03:17.470 --> 00:03:18.890
The empty ponds stay put.

47
00:03:20.010 --> 00:03:22.050
But is every test you run a new try?
